# Lines of code

https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/external/interfaces/IKatanaGovernance.sol#L119
https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/external/libraries/AuthorizationLib.sol#L11


# Vulnerability details

## Proof of Concept
When we call the `IKatanaGovernance::isAuthorized` function in the `AuthorizationLib::checkPair` function, we pass it an `address[]` (array) instead of an `address` (single element). The signature of the `isAuthorized` function expects a `token` address and an `account` address, not several tokens. Passing a wrong argument like this to the `IKatanaGovernance::isAuthorized` function will always result in a runtime revert.
- Here is the `IKatanaGovernance::isAuthorized` function signature:
```solidity
i   nterface IKatanaGovernance {

        /// ... The other code

@>      function isAuthorized(address token, address account) external view returns (bool);

        /// ... The other code

    }
```
- And here is where me passed the wrong argument in the `AuthorizationLib::checkPair` function:
```solidity
    function checkPair(address governance, address token0, address token1) internal view {
@>      address[] memory tokens = new address[](2);
        tokens[0] = token0;
        tokens[1] = token1;
        // @audit type mismatch
@>      require(IKatanaGovernance(governance).isAuthorized(tokens, msg.sender), "UA");
    }
```

## Impact
The mismatch should result in either a compile-time error or runtime revert, preventing the function from being executed. However, if somehow deployed and bypassed, it could lead to critical security issues.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
```diff
    function checkPair(address governance, address token0, address token1) internal view {
        address[] memory tokens = new address[](2);
        tokens[0] = token0;
        tokens[1] = token1;
-       require(IKatanaGovernance(governance).isAuthorized(tokens, msg.sender), "UA");
+       for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokens.length; i++) {
+           require(IKatanaGovernance(governance).isAuthorized(tokens[i], msg.sender), "UA");
+       }
    }
```



## Assessed type

Error

# Lines of code

https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/periphery/base/PeripheryImmutableState.sol#L17


# Vulnerability details

## Proof of Concept
In the `constructor` of the `PeripheryImmutableState` contract, when it was initialized, no validation was performed, so the `_factory` parameter can be an `address(0)` and if it is, even the `governance` variable will be an `address(0)` ; The EVM will treat `address(0)` as if it implements the `IKatanaV3Factory` interface, even though `address(0)` does not have any code or contract associated with it. This will lead to unexpected behavior.
```solidity
    constructor(address _factory, address _WETH9) {
        // @audit missing address(0) check
@>      factory = _factory;
        WETH9 = _WETH9;
@>      governance = IKatanaV3Factory(_factory).owner();
    }
```

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
```diff
    constructor(address _factory, address _WETH9) {
+       require(_factory != address(0), "Invalid factory address");
        factory = _factory;
        WETH9 = _WETH9;
        governance = IKatanaV3Factory(_factory).owner();
    }
```


## Assessed type

Invalid Validation

# Lines of code

https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Factory.sol#L121


# Vulnerability details

## Proof of Concept
The `KatanaV3Factory::_enableFeeAmount` function sets the `fee` parameters and emits an event, but potentially missing input validation to ensure that `fee` is in a valid range. Without validation, unintended or invalid values could be set, which could disrupt the functionality of the contract. For example, the `fee` parameter could be assigned the value zero.
```solidity
    function _enableFeeAmount(uint24 fee, int24 tickSpacing, uint8 feeProtocolNum, uint8 feeProtocolDen) private {
        // @audit lack of non-zero check
        feeAmountTickSpacing[fee] = tickSpacing;
        feeAmountProtocol[fee] = Fraction(feeProtocolNum, feeProtocolDen);
        emit FeeAmountEnabled(fee, tickSpacing, feeProtocolNum, feeProtocolDen);
    }
```

## Impact
- Unexpected Zero-Fee Transactions: Since the fee amount is zero, transactions or pools using this fee setting might execute without any fees. This could result in lost revenue, particularly if the contract is intended to generate income through these fees.

- Economic Exploits and Arbitrage: A zero fee could attract bots or traders exploiting arbitrage opportunities without any cost, potentially draining liquidity from pools and destabilizing markets linked to the contract.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
```diff
    function _enableFeeAmount(uint24 fee, int24 tickSpacing, uint8 feeProtocolNum, uint8 feeProtocolDen) private {
+       require(fee > 0, "Invalid fee");
        feeAmountTickSpacing[fee] = tickSpacing;
        feeAmountProtocol[fee] = Fraction(feeProtocolNum, feeProtocolDen);
        emit FeeAmountEnabled(fee, tickSpacing, feeProtocolNum, feeProtocolDen);
    }
```



