# Lines of code

https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/periphery/V3Migrator.sol#L49
https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/periphery/V3Migrator.sol#L50
https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/periphery/libraries/TransferHelper.sol#L34


# Vulnerability details

## Proof of Concept
The `V3Migrator::migrate` function calls the `TransferHelper::safeApprove` function implemented in the `TransferHelper` library as follow:
```solidity
    function safeApprove(address token, address to, uint256 value) internal {
@>      (bool success, bytes memory data) = token.call(abi.encodeWithSelector(IERC20.approve.selector, to, value));
        require(success && (data.length == 0 || abi.decode(data, (bool))), "SA");
    }
```
As we can see on the line mark with `@>`, this `safeApprove` function use the Openzeppelin's `ERC20.approve` method to change allowance and it didn't manage the case which a non-zero value override a precedent non-zero value, this opens the door to the race condition as mentioned in the Openzeppelin documentation: https://docs.openzeppelin.com/contracts/4.x/api/token/erc20#IERC20-approve-address-uint256-

In addition, this `TransferHelper::safeApprove` function is called in the `V3Migrator::migrate` function without first resetting the old allowance to zero as we cas see:
```solidity
    function migrate(MigrateParams calldata params) external override {

        /// ... The rest of code

        // approve the position manager up to the maximum token amounts
@>      TransferHelper.safeApprove(params.token0, nonfungiblePositionManager, amount0V2ToMigrate);
@>      TransferHelper.safeApprove(params.token1, nonfungiblePositionManager, amount1V2ToMigrate);

        /// ... The rest of code

    }
```
So these calls to the `TransferHelper::safeApprove` function overwrites the current allowance regardless of whether the spender already used it or not. It allows the spender to front-run and spend the amount before the new allowance is set. The attack vector is explained here: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1YLPtQxZu1UAvO9cZ1O2RPXBbT0mooh4DYKjA_jp-RLM/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.m9fhqynw2xvt

The `allowance` should only be cleaned up under certain conditions as implemented at the end of the `V3Migrator::migrate` function: 
```solidity
    function migrate(MigrateParams calldata params) external override {

        /// ... The rest of code

@>      // if necessary, clear allowance and refund dust
        if (amount0V3 < amount0V2) {
        if (amount0V3 < amount0V2ToMigrate) {
@>          TransferHelper.safeApprove(params.token0, nonfungiblePositionManager, 0);
        }

        /// ... The rest of code 

        }
        if (amount1V3 < amount1V2) {
        if (amount1V3 < amount1V2ToMigrate) {
@>          TransferHelper.safeApprove(params.token1, nonfungiblePositionManager, 0);
        }

        /// ... The rest of code

        }
    }
```
Instead, the `allowance` must be reset to zero at any time before a new value is set, to avoid any risk of race condition or front-run.

## Impact
Loss of Funds: A malicious spender with an unlimited allowance can transfer an unlimited number of tokens, resulting in potential loss of all assets from the token holder’s balance

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
```diff
    function migrate(MigrateParams calldata params) external override {

        /// ... The rest of code

        // approve the position manager up to the maximum token amounts
+       TransferHelper.safeApprove(params.token0, nonfungiblePositionManager, 0);
        TransferHelper.safeApprove(params.token0, nonfungiblePositionManager, amount0V2ToMigrate);
+       TransferHelper.safeApprove(params.token1, nonfungiblePositionManager, 0);
        TransferHelper.safeApprove(params.token1, nonfungiblePositionManager, amount1V2ToMigrate);

        /// ... The rest of code

    }
```



## Assessed type

ERC20

# Lines of code

https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-operation-contracts/blob/27f9d28e00958bf3494fa405a8a5acdcd5ecdc5d/src/aggregate-router/modules/katana/v2/V2SwapRouter.sol#L34


# Vulnerability details

## Proof of Concept
In the `V2SwapRouter::_v2Swap` function, we have used the keyword `unchecked` for a block of code which means that all the arithmetic operations in this block will never fail due to an overflow/underflow. However, in this block, certain operations can result in an `underflow`, such as the following operation: `uint256 amountInput = ERC20(input).balanceOf(pair) - reserveInput;`. Over a period of time, it is possible that the value of `reserveInput` will be greater than `ERC20(input).balanceOf(pair)`, resulting in a negative value which will be converted to an `uint256` type due to non-revert on underflow/overflow; and this will lead to a large value of `amountInput` to swap. A malicious user can exploit this by entering a small `amountInput` value but swapping more than is supposed to be done.
```solidity
    function _v2Swap(address[] calldata path, address recipient, address pair) private {
    if (!IKatanaGovernance(KATANA_GOVERNANCE).isAuthorized(path, msg.sender)) revert V2UnauthorizedPath();

@>  unchecked {

        /// ... The other code

        (address input, address output) = (path[i], path[i + 1]);
        (uint256 reserve0, uint256 reserve1,) = IKatanaV2Pair(pair).getReserves();
        (uint256 reserveInput, uint256 reserveOutput) = input == token0 ? (reserve0, reserve1) : (reserve1, reserve0);
        // @audit Possibly underflow
@>      uint256 amountInput = ERC20(input).balanceOf(pair) - reserveInput;
        uint256 amountOutput = KatanaV2Library.getAmountOut(amountInput, reserveInput, reserveOutput);
        (uint256 amount0Out, uint256 amount1Out) =
          input == token0 ? (uint256(0), amountOutput) : (amountOutput, uint256(0));

        /// ... The other code

      }
    }
```
example of a scenario: Ongoing Swap Before Reserves Update

Imagine a scenario where the pair contract is in the middle of a swap operation:
1. Step 1: Initial State
    - The pair holds 1000 tokens of `TokenA` and 500 tokens of `TokenB`.
    - The reserves stored in the pair contract are `reserve0 = 1000` for `TokenA` and `reserve1 = 500` for `TokenB`.
2. Step 2: Swap Initiated
    - A user initiates a swap to trade 100 `TokenA` for `TokenB`.
    - The user transfers 100 `TokenA` into the pair contract, temporarily increasing the balance of `TokenA` to 1100.
    - The pair calculates how much `TokenB` to send to the user based on the reserves and executes the swap.
    - After sending out 45 `TokenB` to the user, the pair now has 1100 `TokenA` and 455 `TokenB` in balance.
3. Step 3: Reserves Update
    - At this moment, before the reserves are updated, the pair still has reserves of `reserve0 = 1000` for `TokenA` and `reserve1 = 500` for `TokenB`, but the balances are `1100 TokenA` and `455 TokenB`.
If the `V2SwapRouter::_v2Swap` function is called at this point, the balance of `TokenB` (455) is lower than its reserve (500). When we calculate `amountInput` using:
```solidity
    uint256 amountInput = ERC20(input).balanceOf(pair) - reserveInput;
```
If `input` is `TokenB`, this calculation would be `455 - 500`, which would normally be reversed due to underflow in Solidity version 0.8.0 and higher, or result in an error; but as we're using `unchecked` to wrap the whole block in this code, no revert will occur and the negative result will be converted to type `uint256` and it's this erroneous value that will be stored as `amountInput` for the swap.

## Impact 
Inappropriate behaviour and theft of funds: a malicious user can use this vulnerability to swap very large amounts of Tokens with a minimal initial investment.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
```diff
    function _v2Swap(address[] calldata path, address recipient, address pair) private {
    if (!IKatanaGovernance(KATANA_GOVERNANCE).isAuthorized(path, msg.sender)) revert V2UnauthorizedPath();

    unchecked {

        /// ... The other code

        (address input, address output) = (path[i], path[i + 1]);
        (uint256 reserve0, uint256 reserve1,) = IKatanaV2Pair(pair).getReserves();
        (uint256 reserveInput, uint256 reserveOutput) = input == token0 ? (reserve0, reserve1) : (reserve1, reserve0);
+       require(ERC20(input).balanceOf(pair) >= reserveInput, "Invalid reserve or balance");
        uint256 amountInput = ERC20(input).balanceOf(pair) - reserveInput;
        uint256 amountOutput = KatanaV2Library.getAmountOut(amountInput, reserveInput, reserveOutput);
        (uint256 amount0Out, uint256 amount1Out) =
          input == token0 ? (uint256(0), amountOutput) : (amountOutput, uint256(0));

        /// ... The other code

      }
    }
```



## Assessed type

Under/Overflow

# Lines of code

https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L714-L735


# Vulnerability details

In the fee growth accumulation logic within the swap function, instead of accumulating fees, the current implementation overwrites the previous fee growth values, potentially leading to a decrease in the total fee growth.

Because the swap function uses an assignment operation instead of addition when updating the fee growth variables. Specifically: [KatanaV3Pool.sol#L715-L716](https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L714-L716)
```solidity
    // update fee growth global
    if (zeroForOne) feeGrowthGlobal0X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128; // <= @audit
    else feeGrowthGlobal1X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128; // <= @audit
```
This code directly assigns the new fee growth value to the global variable, overwriting any previously accumulated fees. The correct behavior should be to add the new fee growth to the existing value.

## Impact
Liquidity providers may receive fewer fees than they are entitled to, resulting in financial losses. 

## Proof of Concept
Assume the following scenario:

   * The pool is initialized with `feeGrowthGlobal0X128 = 100`.
   * A swap occurs with `zeroForOne = true` and `state.feeGrowthGlobalX128 = 50`.
   * After the swap, `feeGrowthGlobal0X128` becomes 50, which is less than its initial value.

This sequence clearly demonstrates that the fee growth can decrease, violating the core invariant of the protocol that fee growth should always increase or remain the same.

[KatanaV3Pool.sol#L714-L735](https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L714-L735)
```solidity
if (zeroForOne) feeGrowthGlobal0X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128; // <= @audit
else feeGrowthGlobal1X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128; // <= @audit

(amount0, amount1) = zeroForOne == exactInput
  ? (amountSpecified - state.amountSpecifiedRemaining, state.amountCalculated)
  : (state.amountCalculated, amountSpecified - state.amountSpecifiedRemaining);

// do the transfers and collect payment
if (zeroForOne) {
  if (amount1 < 0) TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenOut, recipient, uint256(-amount1));

  uint256 balance0Before = balance0();
  IKatanaV3SwapCallback(msg.sender).katanaV3SwapCallback(amount0, amount1, data);
  require(balance0Before.add(uint256(amount0)) <= balance0(), "IIA");
} else {
  if (amount0 < 0) TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenOut, recipient, uint256(-amount0));

  uint256 balance1Before = balance1();
  IKatanaV3SwapCallback(msg.sender).katanaV3SwapCallback(amount0, amount1, data);
  require(balance1Before.add(uint256(amount1)) <= balance1(), "IIA");
}
```
Instead of accumulating the fees, the code is overwriting the previous values.

## Tools Used
VsCode

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The fee growth accumulation logic should be changed to add the new fee growth to the existing value instead of overwriting it. 
```diff
- if (zeroForOne) feeGrowthGlobal0X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128;
- else feeGrowthGlobal1X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128;
+ if (zeroForOne) feeGrowthGlobal0X128 += state.feeGrowthGlobalX128;
+ else feeGrowthGlobal1X128 += state.feeGrowthGlobalX128;
```



## Assessed type

Math

# Lines of code

https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L723-L735
https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L734


# Vulnerability details

In the KatanaV3Pool contract, in the swap function, when performing a swap where `token1` is being exchanged for `token0` (`zeroForOne` is false), the function incorrectly checks the balance of `token0` instead of `token1`. This allows users to drain the pool of `token0` without providing the correct amount of `token1`.

In the else branch of the `zeroForOne` condition, the balance check uses `balance0()` instead of `balance1()`: [KatanaV3Pool.sol#L734](https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L734)
```solidity
    require(balance1Before.add(uint256(amount1)) <= balance0(), "IIA");
```
This means that for swaps where `token1` is being provided, the contract is not verifying if the correct amount of `token1` has been transferred to the pool. Instead, it's checking the balance of `token0`, which is irrelevant for this part of the swap.

## Impact
* Draining of `token0` from the pool without providing the correct amount of `token1`.
* Manipulation of the pool's price and liquidity.
* Unfair profit for attackers at the expense of other liquidity providers.
* Potential complete breakdown of the pool's economic model.

It directly affects the core functionality of the pool and can lead to significant financial losses.

## Proof of Concept
Consider the following scenario

1. The pool starts with 1000 of `token0` and 1000 of `token1`.
2. An attacker initiates a swap to exchange 100 of `token1` for `token0`.
3. The swap function calculates that the attacker should receive 98 of `token0` (assuming a 2% fee).
4. In the [katanaV3SwapCallback](https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L733), the attacker's contract doesn't transfer any `token1` to the pool.
5. The swap succeeds because the check is against `balance0()` instead of `balance1()`.
6. The pool now has 902 of token0 and still 1000 of `token1`, creating an imbalance.

KatanaV3Pool.sol#
```solidity
if (zeroForOne) {
  if (amount1 < 0) TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenOut, recipient, uint256(-amount1));

  uint256 balance0Before = balance0();
  IKatanaV3SwapCallback(msg.sender).katanaV3SwapCallback(amount0, amount1, data);
  require(balance0Before.add(uint256(amount0)) <= balance0(), "IIA");
} else {
  if (amount0 < 0) TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenOut, recipient, uint256(-amount0));

  uint256 balance1Before = balance1();
  IKatanaV3SwapCallback(msg.sender).katanaV3SwapCallback(amount0, amount1, data);
  require(balance1Before.add(uint256(amount1)) <= balance0(), "IIA"); // <-- Bug here
}
```
In the balance check for the non-zeroForOne case. The condition is checking `balance0()` instead of `balance1()`, which could lead to inconsistent fee growth calculations and potentially allow for manipulation of the pool's state.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The balance check in the else branch should be corrected to use `balance1()` instead of `balance0()`.
```diff
if (zeroForOne) {
  if (amount1 < 0) TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenOut, recipient, uint256(-amount1));

  uint256 balance0Before = balance0();
  IKatanaV3SwapCallback(msg.sender).katanaV3SwapCallback(amount0, amount1, data);
  require(balance0Before.add(uint256(amount0)) <= balance0(), "IIA");
} else {
  if (amount0 < 0) TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenOut, recipient, uint256(-amount0));

  uint256 balance1Before = balance1();
  IKatanaV3SwapCallback(msg.sender).katanaV3SwapCallback(amount0, amount1, data);
-  require(balance1Before.add(uint256(amount1)) <= balance0(), "IIA");
+  require(balance1Before.add(uint256(amount1)) <= balance1(), "IIA");
}
```


## Assessed type

Token-Transfer

# Lines of code

https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L714-L740


# Vulnerability details

KatanaV3Pool contract contains vulnerability in its fee growth accounting mechanism within the swap function. This issue leads to inconsistent fee tracking, causing incorrect fee distributions to liquidity providers and compromising the economic integrity of the protocol.

The bug occurred due to an oversimplified update of the fee growth global variables in the swap function. The current implementation only updates one of the fee growth variables based on the swap direction (`zeroForOne`), without considering that fees might accrue for both tokens in a single swap operation.

In [KatanaV3Pool.sol#L714-L740](https://github.com/ronin-chain/katana-v3-contracts/blob/03c80179e04f40d96f06c451ea494bb18f2a58fc/src/core/KatanaV3Pool.sol#L714-L740)
```solidity
// update fee growth global
if (zeroForOne) feeGrowthGlobal0X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128;
else feeGrowthGlobal1X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128; // <= @audit

(amount0, amount1) = zeroForOne == exactInput
  ? (amountSpecified - state.amountSpecifiedRemaining, state.amountCalculated)
  : (state.amountCalculated, amountSpecified - state.amountSpecifiedRemaining);

// do the transfers and collect payment
if (zeroForOne) {
  if (amount1 < 0) TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenOut, recipient, uint256(-amount1));

  uint256 balance0Before = balance0();
  IKatanaV3SwapCallback(msg.sender).katanaV3SwapCallback(amount0, amount1, data);
  require(balance0Before.add(uint256(amount0)) <= balance0(), "IIA");
} else {
  if (amount0 < 0) TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenOut, recipient, uint256(-amount0));

  uint256 balance1Before = balance1();
  IKatanaV3SwapCallback(msg.sender).katanaV3SwapCallback(amount0, amount1, data);
  require(balance1Before.add(uint256(amount1)) <= balance1(), "IIA");
}

// transfer protocol fees to the treasury
if (state.protocolFee > 0) {
  TransferHelper.safeTransfer(tokenIn, IKatanaV3Factory(factory).treasury(), state.protocolFee);
}
```
This approach fails to accurately track fee growth for both tokens, as it overwrites the fee growth of one token without considering potential fee accrual for that token during the swap.

let's say you

1. Initialize a `KatanaV3Pool` with some liquidity.
2. Perform a swap from `token0` to `token1` (`zeroForOne = true`) that generates fees.
3. Immediately after, perform a swap from `token1` to `token0` (`zeroForOne = false`) that also generates fees.
4. Check the values of `feeGrowthGlobal0X128` and `feeGrowthGlobal1X128`.

You'll notice that after step 3, `feeGrowthGlobal1X128` will be overwritten with the value of `state.feeGrowthGlobalX128`, effectively erasing any fee growth for `token1` that occurred in step 2.

## Impact
Liquidity providers may receive inaccurate fee amounts, leading to unfair distribution of rewards.

## Proof of Concept
1. Alice initializes a `KatanaV3Pool` with some liquidity for `token0` and `token1`.
2. performs a swap from `token0` to `token1` (`zeroForOne = true`), generating fees.
3. Charlie immediately performs a swap from `token1` to `token0` (`zeroForOne = false`), also generating fees.
4. The pool's `feeGrowthGlobal1X128` is now incorrect, as it has been overwritten by Charlie's swap without accounting for the fees generated in Bob's swap.

Illustration.
```solidity
// After Bob's swap
uint256 feeGrowth0 = pool.feeGrowthGlobal0X128();
uint256 feeGrowth1 = pool.feeGrowthGlobal1X128();

// Charlie's swap
pool.swap(recipient, false, amountSpecified, sqrtPriceLimitX96, data);

// After Charlie's swap
uint256 newFeeGrowth0 = pool.feeGrowthGlobal0X128();
uint256 newFeeGrowth1 = pool.feeGrowthGlobal1X128();

// newFeeGrowth1 has been overwritten, losing the fees from Bob's swap
assert(newFeeGrowth1 > feeGrowth1); // This assertion may fail
```

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider implementing the following changes

```diff
- if (zeroForOne) feeGrowthGlobal0X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128;
- else feeGrowthGlobal1X128 = state.feeGrowthGlobalX128;
+ uint256 feeDelta0 = zeroForOne ? state.feeGrowthGlobalX128 - feeGrowthGlobal0X128 : 0;
+ uint256 feeDelta1 = zeroForOne ? 0 : state.feeGrowthGlobalX128 - feeGrowthGlobal1X128;
+ feeGrowthGlobal0X128 += feeDelta0;
+ feeGrowthGlobal1X128 += feeDelta1;
```


